  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Signed and numbered by the author of limited-edition of 350.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Volumes 2, 3 and 4 with partially detached spine with exposed stitched binding (Japanese bookbinding).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Issued in slipcase. Each volume has individual book jacket. Slipcase and colophon numbered.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"The images are by Jessica Mitrani in collaboration with Alex Czetwertynski; the illustrations are by Renata Abbade; the translation is by Nikki Columbus; the texts are by Jessica Mitrani. The binding, folded and sewn to the thread, was made by hand in the bookbinding workshop of Arte Dos Gráfico. The book was printed in the lithography workshops of Arte Dos Gráfico during the silent pandemic days of October and November 2021 under the care of Germán Escobar, Junior Curator of the MAMBO, and Luis Ángel Parra and María Eugenia Niño directors of the Taller Arte Dos Gráfico.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="505" ind1="8" ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">1. Soñe que el paisaje me miraba = I dreamt that the landscape was looking at me (contains the text La Parábola del Artista-Profeta by Eugenio Viola, Chief Curator of the MAMBO) -- 2. Primera parte. El planeta es un contagio perpetuo = First part. The planet is a perpetual contagion. First part -- 3. Segunda parte. Territorio = Second part. Territory-- 4. Tercera parte. La naturaleza = Third part. Nature.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="546" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Text in Spanish and English.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Handmade Artist's Book by Jessica Mitrani (Barranquilla, Colombia 1968). Jessica Mitrani is a New York-based multimedia artist and filmmaker. In 2021, her three-channel video installation titled "I Dreamt the Landscape Was Looking at Me" was exhibited at the Bogota Museum of Modern Art - MAMBO. The art piece is trilogy of short videos -Landscape, Nature, and Territory- where Mitrani reflected on human alienation from the environment, a long-standing theme in her feminist decolonial thought. ""I Dreamt the Landscape Was Looking at Me" is an opera conceived as a hallucinatory loop and divided in three interconnected acts. It employs divergent, nonlinear narratives, intertwining humor, absurdity, melodrama, and psychoanalytic tropes by recontextualizing, quotidian gestures, scenes, and objects in an oneiric realm." --Vol. 1, page [2].</subfield>
  </datafield>
